A||||12||Ken Wittlief||anybody familiar with hondas continuously variable tranny?||||1||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||06-28-2001||01:46 PM||||I pulled this Q out of another thread to post as its own subject:<P>does anyone know the story on hondas continiously variable transmission?!<BR>I caught wind of it a few years back but never really looked into it. I think its only on one car - the DelSol maybe.<P>anyone know how it works internally? also does the engine stay at one RPM while the tranny varies to control vehicle speed?<P>sounds like a major innovation but I have'nt never heard a word on it from anywhere.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||06-28-2001||01:54 PM||||CVTs are a *very* intresting technology, Honda did have a prototype (civic I belive) that used a single STEEL segmented belt, and a pair of essentially variable-geometry pulleys.<P>The system worked well, just let the engine hit 5500rpm (or rather, it's horsepower peak) and then drive the car at whatever ration you can to achive max acceleration.<P>CVTs haven't made it to races yet, but there<BR>are several 8 and 12 belt systems out there in F1 cars.  Yes, they're quicker than the semi-automatic "BANG" shifting fixed-ratio gearboxes  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> something like 1sec/lap (substantial, though I do not recall the track)<P>The problem with automotive applications seems to be building a single belt unit that can handle a large ammount of torque...<P>a 1.6litre honda engine reportedly is hard on it....<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||06-28-2001||02:20 PM||||thats gotta make for an interesting contol algorythm - you step the pedal to the floor  - the engine revs to red line, then the tranny varied up to speed?<P> when you back off the pedal it has to lower engine rpms and then what? increase drive ratio to hold speed? back off ratio too? hold ration steady?<P>  in a car pedal = hp to the wheels and the speed of the car is not directly a function of pedal position - I wonder how they mapped the pedal to engine speed AND tranny ratio in those cars?<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tweek||06-28-2001||06:37 PM||||Interesting...  about 10 years ago my dad got most of the way through designing a similar device for bicycles, though after a while I think he lost interest.||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||06-29-2001||06:52 AM||||It's actually about twenty seven times simpler than the control algorythems in every 'F1' optioned Ferrari F355, and now F360 Modena...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Which is to say, it's still fairly involved, but there are just 4th or 5th order mathematical curve fitting models here, and they're not very dynamic. (and you can do wonders with angular sheer &lt;torque&gt; sensors and closed loop monitoring.)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||MrPBody||06-29-2001||09:23 AM||||Is this a grown-up version of the DAF Variomatic?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||06-29-2001||10:44 AM||||we had a drill press at the last company I worked for that had a variable speed pully settup - the top was missing so you could see the pulleys move when you pulled the level from slow to fast - the motor ran at a constant speed and the chuck went from 300 RPM to somthing like 3000 RPM.<P>  a great application of this would be if you could put a flywheel on a bicycle - you could store up energy on a downhill by spinning up the flywheel to brake your speed, when release it to climb the next hill.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shaft||06-29-2001||03:15 PM||||The company I work for (LuK Inc.) has developed in conjunction with Audi a CVT for the A4 and 2002 A6 2.8. The A4 CVT will be in the US at the end of this year. I don't know the performance numbers on the A4, but the A6 is 1.3 sec. quicker 0-60 and goes 2.2 miles farther per gallon of gas with the CVT as opposed to the regular 5 speed auto, and is 0.1 sec faster to 60 and 0.5 mpg more than the 5 speed manual. The problem with the Honda CVT is that it can't handle that much torque (only around 100 ft/lbs) but the Audi one is the first one that can handle larger amounts of torque (186 ft.lbs). CVT's have been around for a long time and were even the basis for the first automatics in the early 1900's. They have also been used in mopeds for a long time. The only differance is that the technology is advancing to the point where it can be used with higher torque engines. <P>I have many articles about the new CVT with pictures of the unit and it describes how it works. The only problem is I have read it many times and I still have no clue how it works. (The concept is simple, understanding how the components work is confusing) I'm not sure what magazines some of these are from, but Car & Driver did an article on it a while ago. It detailed how they work, why they're better, and showed a few different concepts. Other companies are developing their own CVT also. (Mazda/Ford and possibly GM)<P>*All the info here is public info that I have compiled from articles in separate magazines.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Shaft (edited 06-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||pHoOl||06-30-2001||12:24 AM||||the new Saturn SUVs will have that kind of tranny...<P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||06-30-2001||02:01 PM||||If you want to see what one works like,,,, go find a snow mobile. Most of them use esentially the same idea and you can actually see all the parts. <P>The car ones use a steel belt and have a bunch of things added on but the basic idea is the same and has been in use for decades.||||reg||1
Z||000010||filthyscarecrow||06-30-2001||07:18 PM||||i know there were a few cars in detroit at the FSAE competition that were using CVT's. the problem that they were having though is keeping the revs up in the corners. it looked like they were goign into the corners both on the brakes and the gas, trying to keep the CVT on the verge ov pulling so that they'd begin acclerating coming out of the corner. if they didn't do that, there was a huge lag in the time that the tranny took to "spool up" and start pulling the car. so it takes a totally different (and seemingly unnatural) driving style to go fast with one. we had considered one instead of the starnadrt chain driven 6 speed that comes on the R6 but there were both significant downfalls in rotational inertia (a CVT would have weighed like 3 times more than the 6speed) and machining time (a good 200 extra hours in the machine shop). i don't see race cars going with them anytime soon...||||reg||1
Z||000011||Shaft||07-03-2001||01:29 PM||||I just read in the June Car & Driver that the next Nissan Skyline GT-R (which will be brought to the U.S.) is gonna be a 400 hp twin turbo v-6, all wheel drive and a six speed. THE INTERESTING PART: "Strong rumors suggest a CVT option with an eight speed Tiptronic-like function." If this is true this will be the most advanced CVT to date considering current ones can only hold around 185-200 ft/lbs of torque. <P>The Tiptronic-like fnction s also what Audi is doing to the A4. European drivers hate automatic transmissions and in order to get them to buy the CVT they have a programmed an "auto-stick" that has six pre-programed gear ratios for the CVT so it can be driven like a regular manual.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Leadfoot||07-03-2001||06:43 PM||||Isn't the CVT based on snowmobile power transfer technology?  If not, it sure sounds like it.||||reg||1
